    </category>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q01</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q01</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.STAT.DVD.3</p><p>A relative frequency table shows Group A: yes 40%, no 60%. How long should the yes segment be in a 100% segmented bar?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[60% of the bar]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[60% represents the no category.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[100% of the bar]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[The whole bar represents all responses, not one category.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[20% of the bar]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[20% is not listed in the table.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[40% of the bar]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Segment length matches the relative frequency for that category.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q02</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q02</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.STAT.DVD.3</p><p>Why are relative frequencies useful when comparing two groups of different sizes?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[They compare proportions instead of raw counts]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Proportions make comparisons fair when totals differ.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[They remove the need for categories]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Categories are still compared.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[They make every group have the same raw count]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Relative frequency changes the form of comparison, not the actual counts.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[They prove one category caused another]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Relative frequencies do not establish causation.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q03</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q03</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.STAT.DVD.3</p><p>In a segmented bar graph, what should the total length of each bar represent?</p><p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q03_visual.png" alt="Statistics visual supports graph, table, distribution, or data-display interpretation without adding new assessment facts." style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></p>]]></text>
      <file name="STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q03_visual.png" path="/" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[100% of that group]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Each segmented bar represents the whole group split into category parts.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[The largest category only]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[The largest category is one segment, not the whole bar.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[The raw count in the smallest group]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[The bar is scaled to the group total, usually 100%.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[The number of categories squared]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[The number of categories does not determine total bar length.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q04</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q04</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.STAT.DVD.3</p><p>A table shows Grade 9: walk 30%, bus 50%, car 20%. Which segment should be longest?</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Walk]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Walk is 30%, smaller than bus.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Bus]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Bus has the largest relative frequency at 50%.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[Car]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Car is 20%, the smallest of the three.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[All segments are equal]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[The percentages are not equal.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
  <question type="multichoice">
    <name>
      <text>STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q05</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q05</p><p><strong>MLA Standard:</strong> MLA.STAT.DVD.3</p><p>A segmented bar graph uses the same category order in every bar. Why is this important?</p><p><img src="@@PLUGINFILE@@/STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q05_visual.png" alt="Statistics visual supports graph, table, distribution, or data-display interpretation without adding new assessment facts." style="max-width:100%;height:auto;" /></p>]]></text>
      <file name="STAT_U01_L06_GP_Q05_visual.png" path="/" encoding="base64"></file>
    </questiontext>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.3333333</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <single>true</single>
    <shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers>
    <answernumbering>abc</answernumbering>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[It changes the relative frequencies]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Order does not change the data values.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[It makes every segment equal]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Segment size still depends on percentage.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="100" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[It makes comparisons across groups clearer]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Consistent category order prevents misreading segment positions.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
    <answer fraction="0" format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[It removes the need for labels]]></text>
      <feedback format="html">
        <text><![CDATA[Labels are still needed to identify categories.]]></text>
      </feedback>
    </answer>
  </question>
</quiz>